  "text": "miştir\n\nAssistant: To find the least positive value of \\( t \\) such that the sequence \\(\\arccos (\\cos \\alpha), \\arccos (\\cos 3 \\alpha), \\arccos (\\cos 11 \\alpha), \\arccos (\\cos t \\alpha)\\) is an arithmetic progression for some \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(0 < \\alpha < \\pi\\), we need to consider the properties of the \\(\\arccos\\) function and the periodicity of the cosine function.\n\nFirst, recall that the \\(\\arccos\\) function returns a value in the range \\([0, \\pi]\\). For any angle \\(\\theta\\), \\(\\arccos(\\cos \\theta) = \\theta \\mod 2\\pi\\) when \\(\\theta\\) is in the range \\([- \\pi/2, \\pi/2]\\), and \\(\\arccos(\\cos \\theta) = \\pi - \\theta\\) when \\(\\theta\\) is in the range \\([\\pi/2, 3\\pi/2]\\), with similar periodicity properties.\n\nGiven the sequence \\(\\alpha, 3\\alpha, 11\\alpha, t\\alpha\\), we need these terms to be in arithmetic progression. Therefore, the difference between consecutive terms must be constant. Let's denote this common difference by \\(d\\). Then we have:\n\n\\[\n3\\alpha - \\alpha = 2\\alpha = d\n\\]\n\\[\nt\\alpha - 3\\alpha = (t-3)\\alpha = d\n\\]\n\nSince both expressions are equal to \\(d\\), we can set them equal to each other:\n\n\\[\n2\\alpha = t\\alpha - 3\\alpha\n\\]\n\\[\n2\\alpha = (t-3)\\alpha\n\\]\n\nAssuming \\(\\alpha \\neq 0\\), we can divide both sides by \\(\\alpha\\):\n\n\\[\n2 = t - 3\n\\]\n\\[\nt = 5\n\\]\n\nThus, the least positive value of \\(t\\) is \\(5\\). We can verify this by checking the periodicity of the \\(\\arccos\\) function. With \\(t = 5\\), the sequence becomes \\(\\alpha, 3\\alpha, 11\\alpha, 5\\alpha\\). The differences are:\n\n\\[\n3\\alpha - \\alpha = 2\\alpha\n\\]\n\\[\n5\\alpha - 3\\alpha = 2\\alpha\n\\]\n\nBoth differences are equal to \\(2\\alpha\\), confirming that the terms are in arithmetic progression.\n\nTherefore, the least positive value of \\(t\\) is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
